This work introduces several themes common to Gordon's oeuvre, including recognition and repetition, time and memory, complicity and duplicity, authorship and authenticity, and darkness and light. By slowing down the film, Gordon highlights the banality and monotony of the original work, forcing the viewer to reevaluate their relationship with the material. The work also raises questions about authorship and authenticity, as the original film is transformed into a new, altered entity.

Furthermore, '24 Hour Psycho' can be seen as a commentary on the way we consume and interact with media. By stretching the film's duration to a full day, Gordon challenges the viewer's attention span and forces them to engage with the material in a more immersive and prolonged manner. This work also blurs the line between art and film, as it transforms a cinematic experience into a durational installation.

